const title       = 'Are Ravens Crows? The Taxonomy Explained';
const description = 'Ravens and crows are closely related but are not the same bird. Both belong to the genus Corvus, but ravens are larger, have a wedge-shaped tail, and form a distinct group within that genus. This page explains the science clearly.';
const path        = '/questions/are-ravens-crows/';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home',      url: '/' },
  { name: 'Questions', url: '/questions/' },
  { name: 'Are Ravens Crows?', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Is a raven a type of crow?', a: 'In the strict taxonomic sense, ravens belong to the genus Corvus — the same genus that includes all true crows. So in the same way that dogs are canids, ravens are corvids, and they share the genus Corvus with crows. But "raven" and "crow" are not interchangeable terms: ravens are larger, have a different tail shape, and are distinguishable from crows in the field. They are distinct species within the same genus, not the same bird.' },
  { q: 'What is the difference between a raven and a crow?', a: 'The key differences between the Common Raven and American or Carrion Crow: size (ravens are roughly twice the mass of crows), tail shape (raven: wedge-shaped; crow: fan-shaped or slightly rounded), bill (raven: much larger and heavier with a curved culmen), voice (raven: deep, hollow "kronk"; crow: harsher caw), and throat hackles (ravens have long, pointed throat hackles; crows have shorter, rounded feathers). Both belong to genus Corvus but are clearly different birds in the field.' },
  { q: 'Are ravens smarter than crows?', a: 'Both ravens and crows are exceptionally intelligent. Ravens show stronger performance in future planning tasks (Kabadayi & Osvath 2017) and social cognition involving cache deception. Crows (specifically New Caledonian Crows) are superior tool manufacturers. American Crows are better-documented for facial recognition (Marzluff et al 2010). "Smarter" depends entirely on what cognitive domain you measure — the two groups are intelligent in overlapping but not identical ways.' },
];
---

