    <h1>Are Ravens Crows? The Taxonomy Explained</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens and crows are not the same bird, but they are closely related. Both belong to the genus <em>Corvus</em> within the family Corvidae. Ravens — particularly the Common Raven (<em>Corvus corax</em>) — are larger, heavier, and structurally distinct from crows, with a wedge-shaped tail and much larger bill. They are different species within the same genus, like lions and tigers are different species within <em>Panthera</em>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Genus Corvus</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The genus <em>Corvus</em> contains approximately 45 species globally, including ravens, crows, rooks, jackdaws, and several birds called "ravens" or "crows" that are not always clearly one or the other by biology alone. The name "raven" is applied to the largest species; "crow" to medium-sized species; "jackdaw" and "rook" to other corvids. These are common names, not strict taxonomic categories — the genus does not divide neatly into "raven species" and "crow species" by any single biological criterion.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>The Common Raven (<em>Corvus corax</em>) is the largest member of the genus. Phylogenetic analysis suggests it is not necessarily more closely related to other ravens than to some crows — the common name "raven" reflects body size and form, not a monophyletic (single common ancestor) grouping.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Key Differences: Ravens vs Crows</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Comparing the Common Raven with the American Crow (<em>Corvus brachyrhynchos</em>) or Carrion Crow (<em>Corvus corone</em>):<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup><sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Size:</strong> Common Raven: 56–69 cm, 700–1,600 g. American Crow: 40–53 cm, 300–600 g. The raven is roughly twice the mass.</li>
      <li><strong>Tail:</strong> Raven: wedge-shaped (central feathers longer than outer feathers), clearly visible in flight. Crow: fan-shaped or slightly rounded — more uniform length across tail feathers.</li>
      <li><strong>Bill:</strong> Raven: large, heavy, with a noticeably curved culmen (top of bill). Crow: lighter, straighter bill.</li>
      <li><strong>Throat hackles:</strong> Raven: long, pointed, shaggy throat feathers. Crow: shorter, more uniform throat feathers.</li>
      <li><strong>Voice:</strong> Raven: deep, resonant, hollow "kronk" or "pruk." Crow: sharper, higher "caw."</li>
      <li><strong>Flight:</strong> Ravens soar extensively on flat wings; crows flap more and soar less.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Can Ravens and Crows Interbreed?</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> There are no documented natural hybrids between ravens and crows. Despite belonging to the same genus, they are reproductively isolated in the wild — differences in size, habitat, behaviour, and vocalisation prevent interbreeding. In captivity, corvid hybrids (between closely related species) have occasionally been produced, but no raven × crow hybrids are documented from natural populations.</p>
  </section>

